// TypeResolution — the Maxon-tier `resolveTypes` pass. Replaces every
// unresolved / named `MaxonType` with a concrete one so that `lowerMaxonToStd`
// (whose `maxonTypeToStdType` panics on `named`/`unresolved`) never sees an
// unresolved type, and keeps the `funcReturnTypes` registry in sync with the
// resolved module so SemanticCheck reads resolved types.
//
// A named type resolves through FOUR sources, in order:
//
//   1. The BUILTIN alias `ExitCode`, recognized by NAME — WITHOUT loading the stdlib
//      (full stdlib type resolution is deferred until programs call stdlib functions).
//      It resolves to `MaxonType.exitCode`, a width-free tag whose unsigned-32-bit
//      width is assigned at the Maxon→Std boundary (see `maxonTypeToStdType`).
//   2. The project's DECLARED STRUCT registry — every `type Point` the parser declared. It
//      resolves to `MaxonType.structRef(id)`, KEEPING the name id, which is the opposite of
//      what an alias does and is the whole distinction between the two: an alias creates no
//      nominal identity, and a `type` is nothing BUT one. `Point` and `Rect` are different
//      types however identical their fields, and the id is what says so.
//   3. The project's DECLARED ENUM/UNION registry — every `enum`/`union` the parser declared. It
//      resolves to `MaxonType.integer` (an enum value is its i64 tag), DROPPING the id like an
//      alias does: nothing downstream needs to know a value was a `Color`, because only `match`
//      inspects an enum and it reads the enum by name from the signature index at parse time.
//   4. The project's RANGED TYPEALIAS registry — every `typealias Score = int(0 to 100)`
//      the parser declared. An alias resolves to its UNDERLYING primitive and nothing
//      more: two aliases over the same range are the same type, because an alias
//      creates no nominal distinction. The range itself is not consulted here — a
//      narrowing conversion into a ranged alias is checked at run time, which is a
//      later milestone.
//
// A named type in none of the four is a USER error (a typo, or a type this slice does not
// have), not a compiler-invariant violation, and it reports E3011 — unpositioned, because the
// parser records no source span for a type REFERENCE (only ops and parameter NAMES carry
// one). See `resolveNamedType` for why an unpositioned diagnostic is the right trade and
// what positioning it would cost.
//
// The integer literal `42` is already typed `integer` (→ i64) by the parser, so it
// needs no resolution.

// The one builtin alias resolved without the stdlib. Named constant, not a bare
// string — the alias spelling is a contract shared with the parser's interner. Grows
// into a builtin-alias table (Character, etc.) when the stdlib cone lands.
let ExitCodeBuiltinName = b"ExitCode"

// Resolve every unresolved/named type in `module`'s function signatures to a
// concrete `MaxonType`, and re-sync each function's resolved return type into
// the project's `funcReturnTypes` registry (SemanticCheck's source of truth).
export function resolveTypes(project Project, module MaxonModule)
	// Index-based so each function is a mutable binding (a `for x in ...` loop
	// var is immutable; field assignment through it is rejected).
	for i in 0 upto module.functions.count() 'eachFunc'
		var func = try module.functions.get(i) otherwise panic("resolveTypes: functions.get OOB at {i}")
		func.maxonReturnType = resolveReturnType(project, ret: func.maxonReturnType)

		var resolvedParams = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		for pt in func.maxonParamTypes 'eachParam'
			resolvedParams.push(resolveType(project, t: pt))
		end 'eachParam'
		func.maxonParamTypes = resolvedParams

		project.funcReturnTypes.upsert(func.name, value: func.maxonReturnType)
		syncResolvedSignature(project, func: func)
	end 'eachFunc'

	checkStructFieldTypes(project)

	// M1 op value-types are all concrete (`integer` literals) — nothing to stamp.
	// Op-type resolution (typed constants whose type is a named alias) grows at M9.
end 'resolveTypes'

// ⭐ **THE THIRD DECLARATION SITE.** A program names a type in exactly three places — a PARAMETER,
// a RETURN type, and a FIELD — and until P1.1a wave 3 this pass walked only the first two. So a
// field declared `as Nonexistent` reached no check at all, and `Parser.fieldStorageType`'s recovery
// value became the FINAL answer: the program compiled clean and the field silently typed itself
// `i64`. A wrong answer, not a crash.
//
// It also made `fieldStorageType`'s own comment false. That comment justified its recovery value by
// pointing here — "E3011, which `TypeResolution` reports against the merged registry, the
// authority... Returning `integer` keeps the op well-formed for the instant before the pipeline's
// error gate throws" — and nothing was reporting it, so there was no instant and no gate. The
// comment described the right design; the implementation never had it. This walk is what makes the
// sentence true, which is why the sentence stayed.
//
// ⚠ **IT VALIDATES AND DOES NOT REWRITE**, and there are two independent reasons — either alone
// would be sufficient:
//   • **Nothing reads a resolved field type.** The parser cannot wait for this pass (an op's type
//     must be concrete when it is EMITTED, and `resolveTypes` never rewrites an op's type), so it
//     resolves a field's storage type itself, through the sweep. A rewrite here would be a
//     mechanism with no consumer.
//   • **The layout object is the ARTIFACT's own.** `commitStructTypes` upserts it by REFERENCE and
//     an artifact is MEMOIZED, so writing through it would mutate a cached parse — the hazard
//     IrBlock's header states for the phi model, reached from a different direction.
// So `resolveType`'s RESULT is discarded and its DIAGNOSTIC is the point. `resolveNamedType` stays
// the single place that knows what a named type denotes; asking it here rather than re-deriving
// "is this name declared?" is what keeps the three declaration sites answering to one authority.
//
// ⚠ **IT CANNOT LIVE AT THE COMMIT POINT**, which is the tempting alternative (`commitStructTypes`,
// where the layouts arrive in declaration order and a Map walk would not be needed).
// `mergeArtifact` folds ONE artifact at a time, so a field naming a type declared in a file folded
// LATER would report a false E3011. The registry is complete only once every artifact is in, and
// that is exactly here.
//
// The walk is over the registry MAP, so its order is the map's rather than the source's — the one
// place this pass's diagnostics are not in declaration order. It is deterministic (the same program
// hashes the same names into the same slots), which is what the byte-identical-output gate needs;
// and a program with two undeclared field types has two independent bugs, not an ordering.
function checkStructFieldTypes(project Project)
	for (_, layout) in project.structTypes 'eachStruct'
		for fieldType in layout.fieldTypes 'eachField'
			_ = resolveType(project, t: fieldType)
		end 'eachField'
	end 'eachStruct'
end 'checkStructFieldTypes'

// Re-sync a function's `funcSignatures` entry with its now-resolved param types.
// The parser recorded the signature with the SAME raw param types it put on
// `func.maxonParamTypes` (both from one param-parse), so the resolved
// `func.maxonParamTypes` above is exactly the concrete type list to write back; the
// param NAMES are untouched (resolution never renames a parameter). A function with
// no registered signature (none at M5 — the parser records one per function it
// builds) is left alone rather than fabricated.
//
// The pre-erasure `paramAggregateNames` column (OPEN #54 Slice B2) is carried STRAIGHT
// THROUGH — it is precisely the union/enum identity that resolving `func.maxonParamTypes`
// destroys (a `named` union collapses to `integer` here), so it must be read off the
// SIGNATURE (which the parser captured before this pass) and never re-derived from the
// now-erased param types.
function syncResolvedSignature(project Project, func IrFunction)
	if not project.funcSignatures.contains(func.name) 'noSig'
		return
	end 'noSig'
	let sig = try project.funcSignatures.get(func.name) otherwise panic("syncResolvedSignature: contains({String.from(func.name)}) true but get failed")
	project.funcSignatures.upsert(func.name, value: FuncSignature.create(sig.paramNames, paramTypes: func.maxonParamTypes, paramAggregateNames: sig.paramAggregateNames))
end 'syncResolvedSignature'

function resolveReturnType(project Project, ret MaxonReturnType) returns MaxonReturnType
	return match ret 'r'
		void gives MaxonReturnType.void
		value(inner) gives MaxonReturnType.value(resolveType(project, t: inner))
	end 'r'
end 'resolveReturnType'

function resolveType(project Project, t MaxonType) returns MaxonType
	match t 'r'
		boolean then return t
		integer then return t
		float then return t
		exitCode then return t
		named(id) then return resolveNamedType(project, id: id)
		unresolved then panic("resolveTypes: `unresolved` reached type resolution — the parser emits no unresolved placeholders at M1")
		// Already resolved, and by the PARSER: `Parser.parseTypeReference` mints a `structRef`
		// directly for `Self` (and for the enclosing type's own name) inside a `type` body, because
		// that mapping is SYNTACTIC — it needs the enclosing declaration and no registry, so the
		// token sweep and the real parse can both make it and cannot drift. This arm is the OTHER
		// half of that: what `resolveNamedType` produces below for a `type` named from outside its
		// own body, arriving back here through a second `resolveTypes` over a re-resolved signature
		// would find it idempotent. Nothing to do either way.
		structRef then return t
		// Already resolved, and by the PARSER: `Parser.parseTypeReference` mints `MaxonType.string`
		// directly for the `String` builtin (syntactically, like `Self` → `structRef`), so it never
		// arrives here as a `named` to resolve. This arm is what makes that concrete type idempotent
		// under a second `resolveTypes` — there is nothing to do.
		string then return t
		// Already resolved, and by the PARSER: `Parser.parseTypeReference`/`emitFunctionRef` mint
		// `MaxonType.function` directly (a function alias re-tagged, or a functionRef's own name), so it
		// never arrives as a `named` to resolve. Idempotent under a second `resolveTypes` — nothing to do.
		function then return t
		// Already concrete, and by the PARSER (P1.6-A): `Parser.parseTypeReference` mints a
		// `typeParameter` for a `T` in scope and a `genericInstance` for a generic-instance alias
		// reference, both directly. There is no substitution at this trivial slice — a `T` stays `T` (an
		// opaque i64) and an instance stays an instance (a struct pointer) — so resolution leaves both
		// untouched, exactly as it leaves `structRef`/`string`/`function`.
		typeParameter then return t
		genericInstance then return t
	end 'r'
end 'resolveType'

// ⭐ **THE TYPE NAMES THE COMPILER OWNS — a user declaration may not bind one (P1.7a slice 2b-ii).** Homed
// here because this is where the coupling lives: `resolveNamedType` below claims `ExitCode` and `HashValue`
// BEFORE it consults `structTypes`/`enumTypes`, and `createEnumRegistry` seeds `Ordering` INTO those
// registries — so under all three names the AUTHORITY's answer is fixed, while the PARSER's own `named`
// resolution (`resolveNamedStruct`, which reads the struct registry and knows nothing of these names) would
// happily follow a user declaration instead. That disagreement is not academic: **MEASURED — a user
// `type HashValue` made `Hashable.hash()`'s witness result a `structRef`, adopted as an owned struct box and
// dropped as one, faulting with 0xC0000005.** shv2 has no namespacing to keep a user's `Ordering` distinct
// from the builtin (the reference compilers do — theirs come from a stdlib MODULE), so the cure is to make
// the collision unrepresentable: `Parser.requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned` refuses any declaration that would
// mint a NOMINAL identity under one of these names, which is what makes the arms below and the registry seed
// the SOLE writers of them. (A RANGED typealias over one is still legal — it erases to the same primitive
// the builtin does, so it cannot disagree; see that function.)
export function isCompilerOwnedTypeName(name ByteArray) returns bool
	return name.equals(ExitCodeBuiltinName) or name.equals(HashValueTypeName) or name.equals(OrderingTypeName)
end 'isCompilerOwnedTypeName'

function resolveNamedType(project Project, id TypeNameId) returns MaxonType
	let name = try project.typeNames.nameOf(id) otherwise panic("resolveTypes: named type id {id} is not in the project interner (merge did not fold this file's interner)")
	if name.equals(ExitCodeBuiltinName) 'isExitCode'
		return MaxonType.exitCode
	end 'isExitCode'

	// ⭐ A COMPILER-SYNTHESIZED RANGED INT ALIAS — `HashValue`, the result type of `Hashable.hash()`. It is in
	// no alias registry (shv2 reads no stdlib, so its `typealias HashValue = int(0 to u32.max)` is not in
	// `project.typeAliases`), and without this arm it is E3011 on every user `hash() returns HashValue`. It
	// resolves to width-free `integer` exactly as a ranged int alias does (`alias.underlying` drops the
	// range) — the u32 mask lives in the synthesized `int.hash` body, not the type. Handled inline like
	// `ExitCode` rather than seeded into `typeAliases`, which `resetMergeTargets` recreates empty per
	// warm-rebuild miss.
	//
	// The NAME SET is `isSynthesizedIntAliasName`'s, not this arm's, because `Parser.rangedAliasTag` reduces
	// the same names at parse time and the two answered differently until it was named once — see that
	// function's header for the `i.hash().hash()` this cost.
	if isSynthesizedIntAliasName(name) 'isSynthesizedIntAlias'
		return MaxonType.integer
	end 'isSynthesizedIntAlias'

	// ⭐ A DECLARED `type` KEEPS ITS NAME ID, and that one line is the difference between the two
	// registries this function consults. An alias RESOLVES AWAY — `Score` becomes `int`, and
	// nothing downstream can tell it was ever written — because an alias creates no nominal
	// distinction. A struct is nothing but a nominal distinction: `p.x` needs to know WHICH type
	// `p` is to find `x`'s slot (`Parser.emitFieldLoad` reads the id back out and asks
	// `structOf`), and two structs with identical fields are still two types. So the id survives,
	// carried by `structRef`.
	//
	// It is tested BEFORE the alias registry only for readability: a name cannot be both — a
	// `typealias` and a `type` of one name would be a duplicate declaration, and (⚠) that is NOT
	// yet diagnosed. It is a real gap, it belongs with the duplicate-`type` gap `commitStructTypes`
	// records, and neither is this rung's.
	// `contains`, not a fetch: the layout itself is not wanted here. Resolution answers WHICH TYPE
	// this name is, and the answer is the id the reference already carries. The fields are the
	// parser's question, asked of the signature index at the use site.
	if project.structTypes.contains(name) 'isStruct'
		return MaxonType.structRef(id)
	end 'isStruct'

	// ⭐ A DECLARED `enum`/`union` RESOLVES AWAY TO `integer`, the OPPOSITE of a struct — and for the
	// same reason an alias does. An enum value IS its i64 tag (int backing) or its float BIT PATTERN
	// (float backing), never a heap box, so the type carries no nominal identity downstream: after
	// resolution nothing needs to know a value was a `Color` rather than a bare int, because the
	// only thing that inspects an enum — `match` — reads the enum by NAME from the whole-program
	// signature index at PARSE time, long before this pass runs. So the id is dropped, exactly as a
	// ranged alias's is. (Float-backed enums resolve to `integer` too: the bits ARE an i64, matched
	// by an i64 compare — see EnumLayout.)
	//
	// The compiler-known `Ordering` resolves HERE rather than through an inline arm of its own, because
	// `createEnumRegistry` seeds it INTO this registry — one registration, every reader, no arm to add.
	if project.enumTypes.contains(name) 'isEnum'
		return MaxonType.integer
	end 'isEnum'

	// A PLAIN ranged alias ("Score") — checked FIRST after the aggregates, and BEFORE the qualified-alias
	// path below, so the common case never pays the qualified split. A plain alias resolves to its underlying
	// primitive: two aliases over the same range are the same type, an alias creates no nominal distinction.
	//
	// ⚠ Bare-name (`lookupInAnyFile`), and that is exactly right HERE rather than a gap: this arm reads
	// only `underlying`, and it DROPS the range — which is the erasure the whole type system rests on.
	// The RANGE is resolved per declaring file, where it is actually enforced (`InsertRangeChecks`).
	// A file to scope by is also genuinely absent on one of the two paths in: `checkStructFieldTypes`
	// resolves a `StructLayout`'s field types, and a layout records no declaring file.
	match project.typeAliases.lookupInAnyFile(name) 'isPlainAlias'
		declared(alias) then return alias.underlying
		undeclared then break 'isPlainAlias'
	end 'isPlainAlias'

	// ⭐ A QUALIFIED ALIAS (P1.6-C) — a base-template inner alias ("Wrapper.Idx", the shared-body form a
	// generic method's `Idx` carries) OR a per-instance alias ("IntWrapper.Idx"). Both ERASE to their
	// underlying primitive here, EXACTLY as a plain ranged alias does — so `maxonTypeToStdType` never sees a
	// `named` residue in a signature or field type. The per-instance one's nominal name survives only in the
	// VALUE column, where the front-end identity checks read it; a signature carries no identity. Reached only
	// for a name that is NEITHER a plain alias nor an aggregate — a rare, dotted name — so the memoized
	// `queryProgramSignatures` fetch and the qualified-name split never touch the plain-alias hot path.
	let signatures = try queryProgramSignatures(project) otherwise panic("resolveNamedType: queryProgramSignatures failed inside resolveTypes — it is computed and memoized by compileToCodeResult before the pipeline runs, so it cannot fail fresh here")
	match signatures.qualifiedAliasOf(name) 'qualified'
		declared(alias) then return alias.underlying
		undeclared then break 'qualified'
	end 'qualified'

	// A name in NONE of the sources is a USER error — a typo (`Scor` for `Score`), a bare
	// sized type (`u8` is not a type in Maxon; a ranged alias over it is), or a
	// user-defined type this slice does not have. It panicked until now, which was a
	// tolerable bargain only while `ExitCode` was the sole legal named type and no
	// source file could plausibly write another. Ranged typealiases ended that: naming a
	// type is now the most ordinary thing a Maxon file does, and a one-character slip in
	// one must not take the compiler down.
	//
	// UNPOSITIONED, exactly like E3001/E3002 — nothing records a source span for a type
	// REFERENCE yet (only ops and parameter NAMES carry one), so there is no line to
	// point at. Positioning it means threading that span from `parseTypeReference`
	// through `FuncSignature` into the registry this pass reads, which is its own piece
	// of work; a bare diagnostic beats a panic until it lands.
	reportDiagnostic(project, diagnostic: Diagnostic.create(ErrorCode.unknownType, message: "Unknown type '{String.from(name)}'"))

	// A RECOVERY type, not a sentinel answer: `PassPipeline.run` gates on diagnostics
	// after EVERY pass and `resolveTypes` IS a pass, so the build throws the moment
	// this pass returns. Nothing downstream ever reads it — it exists only to keep
	// the module structurally well-formed for the instant in between, so that the
	// rest of this pass can go on to report the OTHER unknown types in the file
	// rather than stopping at the first.
	return MaxonType.integer
end 'resolveNamedType'
